From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aditya kale <252700881+kaleaditya28897-linux@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 13:14:30 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] hurd, htl: no nested functions, part 3: the rest (clang has
 none)

Helpers that used nothing of their function, or only what they can be
passed (hurdauth.c, sendto.c, recvmsg.c, intr-msg.c, sigunwind.c,
ptrace.c): static functions, and a macro naming one with its caller's
locals where they were many.

Callbacks given a context: _hurd_priority_which_map (get/setpriority),
_hurd_ctty_input/_output (fd-read.c, fd-write.c, ioctl.c),
_hurd_startup (init-first.c's doinit, ld.so's go) take a context
argument, none of them exported; the sigstate's cancel_hook takes
cancel_hook_arg, a member added at the end of struct hurd_sigstate, as
htl's own cancel hook does (pt-hurd-cond-timedwait.c).  ioctl.c's
message is a type of its own, and send_rpc's context.  spawni.c's
helpers share a context of pointers to __spawni's locals, its lookups a
struct hurd_lookup_ctx (part 2).

Signal handlers, which are given no context: catch-signal.c's throw and
sigtimedwait.c's handler find theirs on the thread's own preemptor list.
A catch's preemptor function (called with the signal thread holding the
sigstate lock, the thread stopped) marks the matched catch, and no other,
the one fired; throw jumps to it.  A wait's preemptor records its
signal; the handler takes the innermost wait whose signal came.

Behaviour is unchanged.  Joist, Phase 5 step 7.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
